and the number of transgenic pups  recovered after the procedure is also  very similar  non-surgical embryo transfer can also be  used to transfer embryos generated by in  vitro fertilization  this graph shows that  the pregnancy rate and the birth rate  for non-surgical embryo transfer  ivf of fresh oocytes from b6 c3f1 female  mice and cryopreserved sperm from b6 e3  f1 males  was performed  fertilized embryos were then cultured in  ksom media to blast assist stage and  transferred into 2.5 days  pseudo-pregnant females  transfer of the ivf derived blastocyst  resulted in about a 60 pregnancy rate  and a 43 percent  birth rate  for ivf  insect can also be used to  transfer cryopreserved embryos here  cryopreserved b6 c3f1 one cell embryos  were cultured to blast assist in ksi  media and transferred again to 2.5 day  pseudo-pregnant cd1 recipients  the transfer of the cryopreserved  embryos resulted in a 50  pregnancy rate and a  39  uh birth rate  for this experiment  non-surgical embryo transfer can also be  used to transfer embryos after esl  injections um this data is courtesy of  the micro injection service at  the jackson laboratories  here c57 black six  blastocysts were injected with jm8 a3 n1  es cells  and  then they were transferred into the 2.5  day pseudo-pregnant  cby b6 f1j recipients  in this experiment the pregnancy rate  from uh nsaid transfers of esl injected  blastocyst was the pregnancy rate was 80  the birth rate was  uh  31  and the rate of the total rate of  chimerism of a number of chimeras  per  the number of pups surviving to weaning  was 54  we've also been able to successfully  transfer  uh  sperm for artificial insemination with  the nsaid device and so here um i'm  showing the  pregnancy rate for this experiment was  roughly 36 percent  and the  picture on the right is a picture of the  result one of the resulting pups from  one of these experiments  uh in in  summary um the  artificial insemination experiment  actually was performed with uh super  ovulated females um  and the uh sperm delivery occurred on  the day after the second  hormone injection and  i'm not going to go over the specifics  of this particular experiment right now  but i will  be happy to cover that later if  anybody's interested  in conclusion  um the nsaid device can successfully  transfer embryos after micro injection  of dna when cultured blast is a stage  um  it can successfully transfer blastocyst  derived from in vitro fertilization  also blastocysts developed from  preserved early stage embryos  it can also transfer esl injected  blastocysts resulting in recovery of  chimeric pups  and it can also be used to transfer  sperm during artificial insemination  resulting in live births  a couple of other uses which we're not  going to be talking about today but  have been  referenced in the literature  is that you can also transfer pathogens  using the  non-surgical embryo transfer device  and other materials  for example con a coded  sepharose beads  drugs can be delivered this way  and a multitude of other materials that  can be used for research  this is a picture of the nsa device um  and the inset device is a teflon  catheter as uh  attached to a hub  which then attaches to a p2 pipette  which is used then for  delivery of specified  volumes of liquid  and as you can see here it is um  individually packaged an ethylene oxide  treated so that it can be used in  sterile environments  and this device actually also comes with  two specula in the ethylene oxide  treated package  which these of those  specula we'll be talking about in a  minute  the onset procedure and device that i'm  about to describe in detail was in was  invented by dr brett speer and michael  green at the university of kentucky  transgenic core facility  they  have described this in the literature  and there are three very simple steps to  this procedure  first of all  load the embryos into the device prepare  the recipient mouse and deliver the  embryos  to prepare the embryos  first the nsaid device is attached to a  p2 pipet  the embryos can then be loaded into the  pipet under  while watching under a microscope  up to 20 embryos can be loaded into the  inside device and 1.8 microliter of  media  the volume is then adjusted to 2  microliters to introduce a small bubble  into the bottom of the tip which helps  hold the liquid into the device  next the recipient mouse is prepared  a suitable female mouse is selected and  she needs to be 2.5 dpc pseudo-pregnant  the mouse is then held into position on  the cage top  a small speculum is inserted into the  vagina and then removed again and then a  larger speculum is inserted into the  vagina  at that point you can visually locate  the cervix  which there's a picture of the way that  the mouse is being held here shown here  and on the next slide you can see a  close-up  of a cd1 mouse  with the larger of the two nsaid specula  inserted and you can  hopefully you can see the cervix in  there and there's a small depression in  the center and that is what you would  aim the inset device at  next a video presentation of the  procedure will be  i've placed a 15 microliter drop of  media  under the lid of this petri dish and  i've loaded my embryos into it  now i'm taking the m set device  and i've set my p2 pipetman to 1.8  microliters  you simply place the  inset device onto the pipe batman  look into your microscope  bring the tip down  and slowly  release the plunger to load  the embryos once you've loaded the inset  then just dial up  to 2.00  microliters on your pipetman  this will introduce a small air bubble  at the tip and that'll help keep your  embryos from wicking out when they touch  the inside of the mouse until you get it  to where you're ready to expel the  embryos so the smaller piece of tubing  is used  to open up the vagina  just insert it briefly  pull it back out  now we're ready to insert the speculum  just uh insert the speculum  adjust your lighting so that you can see  down and you can actually  see  the cervical opening  pick up your inset device  insert into the mouse  until the hub touches  the speculum expel the embryos  remove the inset device  remove the speculum  and the procedure is complete  so um  if you'd like to see the video  demonstration again  um it can be seen on the peritects  website  also on the peritect's website are  complete instructions frequently asked  questions and helpful hints and tips  i can be contacted directly also by the  email address on this slide  i'd like to take a moment to mention a  few of the most important tips that we  have uh first of all removing the nsa  device from the uterine horn without  releasing the pipet plunger  is highly recommended  the nsa device itself was designed as a  single use device and so repeated use  will clog the inset tip with tissue  which could lead to unfavorable results  as always  inspection of the tip is good practice  just to make sure that the tip is not  bent when inserted into the mouse and  also that the  that any material hasn't clogged the tip  please do not use lubricants during this  procedure  they can clog the tip as well and they  are unneeded for  insertion of the specula  and please make sure  to read the nsaid instruction sheet  thoroughly before use  one of the most often asked questions is  which recipient stream should i use  and so we have just we have uh compared  a few different strains  that are used for  recipients as  during non-surgical embryo transfer  and this  graph shows strain dependent pregnancy  rates for embryo transfer methods  the pregnancy rate is shown on the left  and the various different strains are  indicated below  shown here are cd1 mice swiss webster  b6 cba f1s  c3hs  and c57 black six  also we've tested  uh  b6  d2 f1  recipients although not shown on this  graph  i will include the their results as well  what you can see is a comparison between  non-surgical embryo transfer and surgery  for each of these strains  and for the  if you look at the highest pregnancy  rates here you can see that the cd1  females the b6 cba f1 females and the  c3h females all have a very high rate of  pregnancy  the b62 f1s also had an 80  pregnancy rate and so they would be  included in the better group of embryo  recipients  on the next slide you can see strain  dependent birth rates for embryo  transfer methods and here the birth  rates indicated on the left the strain  is indicated on the bottom  and there's a comparison between  non-surgical embryo transfer and surgery  and what you can see here is that the  cd1  females  had the highest rate of the highest  birth rate shown at 46  the b6 g2 f1s also had a 44  birth rate and so we're also very high  so what we've concluded from this is  that many different strains can actually  be used as recipients for embryo  transfer  strains  in the labs are generally chosen for  convenience and ease of use a lot of  people try to use the strains that they  already have  and we've shown that it's possible to do  so  we recommend the 2.5  dpc cd1 pseudopregnant females  for recipients here  mostly for  well for three reasons first of all they  have the height one of the highest  pregnancy rates um also they have the  highest birth rate and they are  extremely docile and easy to handle and  so as such  we prefer to use them here  some of the other strains that have high  birth rates  and pregnancy rates  tend to be a little more  difficult to handle  and a lot of times aren't as good um  aren't as good mothers and so  we prefer to do those to use the cd ones  so some of the advantages of the nsaid  procedure itself  first and foremost it eliminates a  surgical procedure entirely so  there's no pain or distress to the  animals you can see from the video that  the animal is very calm and easy to work  with  and that animal had received no  anesthesia or analgesia  again  for the purposes of a non-surgical  embryo transfer there is no anesthesia  required  there's no post-surgical monitoring  after the procedure is done the animal  is simply returned to the cage and the  procedure is over  one of the most important things  from a technical standpoint is that  there's no surgical training required  for this procedure  learning the procedure is very easy um  it's not something that requires a lot  of technical training and can be very  quickly  implemented in your laboratory  the  non-surgical embryo transfer procedure  satisfies one of the three r's it's a  refinement of a technique it reduces  pain and distress  it's a terrific improvement for animal  welfare  it also reduces regulatory burden  the  need to explain why you need to use a  surgery and how the surgery is going to  be performed and all the  methods that are required to  prepare your lab for a surgical  procedure need not be performed  this is a non-uh  invasive well  non-surgical invasive procedure  it's extremely fast the procedure itself  only takes about a minute from start to  finish then that includes loading the  embryos into the device  which is much faster than trying to  perform a surgical procedure where the  post procedure monitoring even takes a  large chunk of your research day  it's extremely convenient  and there's a significant cost savings  involved especially if you need to  purchase surgical  materials an anesthesia machine  perhaps  training  technicians or researchers to do the  procedures  for a surgical procedure is very cost  intensive  whereas the non-surgical embryo transfer  procedure  is  much less expensive  so  in conclusion  i'd like to take a moment to acknowledge  a few people and  institutions that have helped with this  research  first of all the testing of the  non-surgical embryo transfer device for  esl injected molasses was performed  under contract at the the jackson  laboratory we'd also like to thank kim  kariko for her contributions towards  developing the eliza protocol  and mouse specific